A||||1||theogre||Chip It. Safe Chip Handling.
Z||000000||theogre||04-27-99||10:15 PM||||When reading about installing new, or reseating an old, ECM PROM you often see the warning that you must <b>Never</b> push on the PROM but only on the frame it is in. ((even if the warning isn't there, Don't push on the chip.)) There is actually more to it than that.<p>The PROM chip frame is actually a part of the socketing system for the EPROM. The frame actually supports the chip pins against the socket contacts presure. If you push on the EPROM, or on the wrong part of the frame, you can mash the pins of the chip.<p>There is also a notch in one end of the frame. this notch matches the "notch" in the chip itself. (the chip notch may not be visible when mounted in the frame.) This notch indicates pin 1 of the chip. there is also a coresponding notch, or other mark, on the socket. IF YOU PLUG THE CHIP IN BACKWARD YOU WILL FRY IT! The socket in the ECM is keyed but some twits manage it anyways.<p>BTW for those with computer background.... the chip is a standard 27xx family EPROM. <p>for those without computer background.... The chip is usually an EPROM, Erasable Programable Read Only, memory chip similar to the ROM chip that boots you home computer. (Ok... they used to be ROMs or EPROMs but now there are also "flash" roms....) If you peal off the lable and shine a strong UV light at the window in the chip, you will erase the chip. 
